{"name":"Water Fountain","hex":"#77b6d5","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"water fountain","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mechanical device designed to dispense small quantities of drinking water, usually in a public place such as a school or office.","Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see water,‎ fountain."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/water_fountain"}],"entries":[{"title":"Drinking fountain","blocks":[{"text":"A drinking fountain, also called a water fountain or bubbler, is a fountain designed to provide drinking water. It consists of a basin with either continuously running water or a tap. The drinker bends down to the stream of water and swallows water directly from the stream. Modern indoor drinking fountains may incorporate filters to remove impurities from the water and chillers to lower its temperature. Drinking fountains are usually found in public places, like schools, rest areas, libraries, and grocery stores."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Bubbler.jpg/330px-Bubbler.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drinking_fountain","wikidata":"Q1630622"},{"title":"Water","blocks":[{"text":"Water is an inorganic compound with the chemical formula H2O. It is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance. It is the main constituent of Earth's streams, lakes, and oceans. Water is also the fluid of all known living organisms, in which it acts as a solvent. Water, being a polar molecule, undergoes strong intermolecular hydrogen bonding which is a large contributor to its physical and chemical properties. It is vital for all known forms of life, despite not providing food energy or being an organic micronutrient. Due to its presence in all organisms, its chemical stability, its worldwide abundance, and its strong polarity relative to its small molecular size, water is often referred to as the \"universal solvent\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/H2O_2D_labelled.svg/330px-H2O_2D_labelled.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water","wikidata":"Q283"},{"title":"Fountain","blocks":[{"text":"A fountain, from the Latin \"fons\", meaning source or spring, is a decorative reservoir used for discharging water. It is also a structure that jets water into the air for a decorative or dramatic effect in landscapes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Fountains_Collage.jpg/330px-Fountains_Collage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fountain","wikidata":"Q483453"},{"title":"Color of water","blocks":[{"text":"The color of water varies with the ambient conditions in which that water is present. While relatively small quantities of water appear to be colorless and transparent, pure water has a slight blue color that becomes deeper as the thickness of the observed sample increases. The hue of water is an intrinsic property and is caused by selective absorption and scattering of blue light. Dissolved elements or suspended impurities may give water a different color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Glass_Half_Full_bw_1.JPG/330px-Glass_Half_Full_bw_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Color_of_water","wikidata":"Q5148636"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Water_pumps_in_Durbar_Square.jpg/500px-Water_pumps_in_Durbar_Square.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Water_pumps_in_Durbar_Square.jpg/500px-Water_pumps_in_Durbar_Square.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In 2026, people are still using this drinking fountain built in 570 AD called Manga Hiti in Lalitpur, Nepal","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWater_pumps_in_Durbar_Square.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Nasona_a_via_annia_faustina_1.JPG/500px-Nasona_a_via_annia_faustina_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Nasona_a_via_annia_faustina_1.JPG/1280px-Nasona_a_via_annia_faustina_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Nasona_a_via_annia_faustina_1.JPG/500px-Nasona_a_via_annia_faustina_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The typical drinking fountain in Rome, called nasone","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANasona_a_via_annia_faustina_1.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Bhaktapur-Naga_Pokhari-10-gje.jpg/500px-Bhaktapur-Naga_Pokhari-10-gje.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Bhaktapur-Naga_Pokhari-10-gje.jpg/1280px-Bhaktapur-Naga_Pokhari-10-gje.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Bhaktapur-Naga_Pokhari-10-gje.jpg/500px-Bhaktapur-Naga_Pokhari-10-gje.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ancient dhunge dhara (drinking fountain) of Bhaktapur","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABhaktapur-Naga_Pokhari-10-gje.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Fountain_Snow_Hill_Samuel_Gurney..jpg/500px-Fountain_Snow_Hill_Samuel_Gurney..jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, htt